<section id="s1">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">01</span><h2>What CSS is and how it attaches</h2><span class="tagline">start here</span></div>

  <p><strong>C</strong>ascading <strong>S</strong>tyle <strong>S</strong>heets. A list of rules saying "elements matching this pattern should look like this". Each rule has two halves:</p>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">h1</span> {                    <span class="cm">/* selector — which elements */</span>
  <span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">#0B5D51</span>;       <span class="cm">/* declaration: property + value */</span>
  <span class="at">font-size</span>: <span class="st">2rem</span>;
}                       <span class="cm">/* the { } part is the declaration block */</span></div>

  <h3>Three ways to attach it, one of which you should use</h3>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>External file</b><span>A separate <code>.css</code> file linked from the head. <strong>Use this.</strong> One file styles every page, the browser caches it, and your HTML stays readable.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>&lt;style&gt; in the head</b><span>Rules written inside the HTML file. Fine for a single-page demo or an email template. Doesn't scale.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>style="" attribute</b><span>Inline, on one element. Hard to override, impossible to reuse, and it beats almost everything in the cascade. Avoid except when JavaScript is setting a value at runtime.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — set up a playground</h4>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="p">$</span> mkdir -p ~/code/css-practice && cd ~/code/css-practice
<span class="p">$</span> code .</div>

  <p>Create <code>index.html</code>:</p>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span>
<span class="tg">&lt;html</span> <span class="at">lang</span>=<span class="st">"en"</span><span class="tg">&gt;</span>
<span class="tg">&lt;head&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;meta</span> <span class="at">charset</span>=<span class="st">"UTF-8"</span><span class="tg">&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;meta</span> <span class="at">name</span>=<span class="st">"viewport"</span> <span class="at">content</span>=<span class="st">"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"</span><span class="tg">&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;title&gt;</span><span class="tx">CSS practice</span><span class="tg">&lt;/title&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;link</span> <span class="at">rel</span>=<span class="st">"stylesheet"</span> <span class="at">href</span>=<span class="st">"style.css"</span><span class="tg">&gt;</span>
<span class="tg">&lt;/head&gt;</span>
<span class="tg">&lt;body&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;h1&gt;</span><span class="tx">CSS practice</span><span class="tg">&lt;/h1&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;p</span> <span class="at">class</span>=<span class="st">"lead"</span><span class="tg">&gt;</span><span class="tx">A paragraph to style.</span><span class="tg">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
  <span class="tg">&lt;div</span> <span class="at">class</span>=<span class="st">"box"</span><span class="tg">&gt;</span><span class="tx">Box</span><span class="tg">&lt;/div&gt;</span>
<span class="tg">&lt;/body&gt;</span>
<span class="tg">&lt;/html&gt;</span></div>

  <p>And <code>style.css</code> next to it:</p>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">body</span> {
  <span class="at">font-family</span>: <span class="st">system-ui, sans-serif</span>;
  <span class="at">margin</span>: <span class="st">2rem</span>;
}

<span class="tg">.box</span> {
  <span class="at">background</span>: <span class="st">#E4EEEB</span>;
  <span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">1rem</span>;
}</div>

  <p>Open it with Live Server. Everything from here assumes these two files are in front of you.</p>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">The meta viewport line is not optional</p><p>Without <code>&lt;meta name="viewport"&gt;</code>, a phone pretends to be a 980px-wide desktop and renders your page zoomed out and unreadable. Every media query you write will be ignored.</p><p>It is the single most common reason a "responsive" site isn't. It's in the snippet above — leave it there.</p></div>

