<!-- ================= 02 ================= -->
<section id="s2">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">02</span><h2>Selectors — querying the tree</h2><span class="tagline">CSS is a tree query language</span></div>

  <p>A selector is a pattern matched against the DOM. That's worth holding onto — CSS isn't styling <em>text</em>, it's selecting <em>nodes</em>, and everything about how selectors combine follows from the tree.</p>

  <h3>The basics</h3>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>p</b><span>Every <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element. Called a type selector.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>.lead</b><span>Every element with <code>class="lead"</code>. Your everyday tool.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>#intro</b><span>The element with <code>id="intro"</code>. Works, but see the specificity warning in section 03.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>*</b><span>Everything. Mostly used in resets.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>[type="email"]</b><span>Attribute selector. Very useful for forms: <code>input[required]</code>, <code>a[href^="https"]</code>.</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Combining them — this is where the tree matters</h3>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">nav a</span>        { }   <span class="cm">/* any a that is a DESCENDANT of a nav, any depth */</span>
<span class="tg">nav &gt; a</span>      { }   <span class="cm">/* only a DIRECT CHILD of a nav */</span>
<span class="tg">h2 + p</span>       { }   <span class="cm">/* a p immediately AFTER an h2, same parent */</span>
<span class="tg">h2 ~ p</span>       { }   <span class="cm">/* every p after an h2, same parent */</span>
<span class="tg">a.button</span>     { }   <span class="cm">/* an a that ALSO has class button — no space! */</span>
<span class="tg">.card, .panel</span>{ }   <span class="cm">/* either one — a comma means OR */</span></div>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">The space is a real operator</p><p><code>a.button</code> and <code>a .button</code> mean completely different things. The first is "an anchor with class button". The second is "anything with class button, inside an anchor".</p><p>One accidental space is a genuinely common bug and it produces no error — the rule just silently matches nothing.</p></div>

  <h3>Pseudo-classes — state, not structure</h3>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>:hover</b><span>Mouse is over it. Remember it doesn't exist on touch devices.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>:focus</b><span>Keyboard or click focus. <strong>Never remove focus styles</strong> without replacing them — keyboard users navigate by them.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>:focus-visible</b><span>Focus, but only when the browser thinks a focus ring should show — keyboard yes, mouse click no. Use this instead of <code>:focus</code> for outlines.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>:first-child / :last-child</b><span>Position among siblings.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>:nth-child(2n)</b><span>Every second one. <code>odd</code> and <code>even</code> also work.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>:not(.active)</b><span>Everything except. Takes a selector.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>:disabled / :checked / :invalid</b><span>Form states, straight from the DOM. Style a checked checkbox's label with no JavaScript at all.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — style form state with zero JavaScript</h4>
    <p>Add an input to your HTML and this to your CSS. Type something invalid and watch it react as you type.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">input:invalid</span>  { <span class="at">border-color</span>: <span class="st">#E06C4F</span>; }
<span class="tg">input:focus-visible</span> { <span class="at">outline</span>: <span class="st">3px solid #F5C518</span>; }</div>

  <div class="quiz">
    <div class="q"><b>Q1</b>What's the difference between <code>.card p</code> and <code>.card &gt; p</code>?</div>
    <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="a">
      <p><code>.card p</code> matches any paragraph <em>anywhere</em> inside a card, however deeply nested. <code>.card &gt; p</code> matches only paragraphs that are <em>direct children</em> of the card.</p>
      <p>If a card contains <code>&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;…&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code>, the first selector matches that paragraph and the second doesn't.</p>
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 02 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 03 ================= -->
<section id="s3">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">03</span><h2>The cascade, specificity, inheritance</h2><span class="tagline">why my rule isn't applying</span></div>

  <p>Two rules target the same element and say different things. Which wins? This is the question behind most CSS frustration, and the answer is a fixed procedure — not a mystery.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">The order the browser decides in</p>
    <p><strong>1. Importance</strong> — <code>!important</code> beats normal. Rare and best avoided.</p>
    <p><strong>2. Specificity</strong> — a more specific selector beats a less specific one.</p>
    <p><strong>3. Source order</strong> — if specificity ties, the one written <em>later</em> wins.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Specificity, counted properly</h3>
  <p>Count three numbers for each selector — <strong>ids, classes, elements</strong> — and compare left to right.</p>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>p</b><span><code>0-0-1</code> — one element</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>.lead</b><span><code>0-1-0</code> — one class. Beats <em>any</em> number of elements.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>p.lead</b><span><code>0-1-1</code></span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>nav ul li a</b><span><code>0-0-4</code> — still loses to a single class</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>#intro</b><span><code>1-0-0</code> — beats any number of classes</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>style="" attribute</b><span>Beats all selectors. Only <code>!important</code> overrides it.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>The comparison is <strong>not</strong> a sum. <code>0-1-0</code> beats <code>0-0-9</code>, because the class column is compared first and eleven elements still lose to one class.</p>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">Why you should mostly stick to classes</p><p>If everything is a single class, everything is <code>0-1-0</code> and the winner is simply whichever came last — which is easy to reason about and easy to override.</p><p>Mix in ids and long descendant chains and you get a specificity war: the only way to beat <code>#sidebar ul li a</code> is something even more specific, and soon somebody reaches for <code>!important</code> and the file becomes unmaintainable. This is not a hypothetical; it's how most legacy stylesheets got that way.</p></div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — see it happen</h4>
    <p>Put both of these in your CSS, in this order, and give a paragraph <code>class="lead"</code> and <code>id="intro"</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">#intro</span>  { <span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">red</span>; }     <span class="cm">/* 1-0-0 */</span>
<span class="tg">.lead</span>   { <span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">blue</span>; }    <span class="cm">/* 0-1-0 — written later, still loses */</span></div>
  <p>It's red. Now open DevTools, find the element, and look at the Styles pane: the blue rule is there with a <strong>line through it</strong>. That strikethrough is the browser telling you exactly which rule lost, and it's the fastest debugging tool in CSS.</p>

  <h3>Inheritance</h3>
  <p>Some properties pass down the tree to descendants automatically. Most don't.</p>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>Inherited</b><span>Text-related things: <code>color</code>, <code>font-family</code>, <code>font-size</code>, <code>line-height</code>, <code>text-align</code>, <code>visibility</code>.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>Not inherited</b><span>Box-related things: <code>margin</code>, <code>padding</code>, <code>border</code>, <code>width</code>, <code>background</code>, <code>display</code>.</span></div>
  </div>
  <p>Which is why setting <code>font-family</code> on <code>body</code> styles the whole page, but setting <code>padding</code> on <code>body</code> does not pad every element. The rule of thumb: if inheriting would obviously be useful, it probably inherits.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">Two modern helpers worth knowing</p><p><code>:where(.card)</code> matches like <code>.card</code> but contributes <strong>zero specificity</strong>. Useful for defaults that should be trivially overridable.</p><p><code>:is(h1, h2, h3)</code> is shorthand for a list, taking the specificity of its most specific argument. Saves a lot of repetition.</p></div>

  <div class="quiz">
    <div class="q"><b>Q2</b>You write <code>.button { background: blue }</code> but the button stays grey. Give the three things to check, in order.</div>
    <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="a">
      <p><strong>1.</strong> Does the selector match at all? Check for a typo, a missing class on the element, or the space bug from section 02. DevTools will show the rule if it matched.</p>
      <p><strong>2.</strong> Is a more specific rule winning? Look for the strikethrough in the Styles pane.</p>
      <p><strong>3.</strong> Is an equally specific rule later in the file overriding it?</p>
      <p>Almost every "CSS is broken" moment is one of these three, and DevTools answers all of them in about five seconds.</p>
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 03 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 04 ================= -->
<section id="s4">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">04</span><h2>The box model</h2><span class="tagline">the most important section</span></div>

  <p>Every element the browser draws is a rectangle. That rectangle has four nested layers, and knowing them is the difference between placing things deliberately and nudging numbers until it looks right.</p>

  <figure class="board">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 780 340" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="The box model: content at the centre, surrounded by padding, then border, then margin.">
      <rect x="90" y="26" width="500" height="266" fill="#F7E9B8" stroke="#8A6D08" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/>
      <text x="102" y="46" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#8A6D08" font-weight="600">margin</text>

      <rect x="150" y="70" width="380" height="178" fill="#0F1A18"/>
      <text x="162" y="90" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#F5C518" font-weight="600">border</text>

      <rect x="172" y="98" width="336" height="122" fill="#CDE3DC"/>
      <text x="184" y="118" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#0B5D51" font-weight="600">padding</text>

      <rect x="212" y="130" width="256" height="58" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#0B5D51" stroke-width="2"/>
      <text x="340" y="164" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans Condensed, sans-serif" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="#0F1A18">CONTENT</text>

      <g font-family="IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif" font-size="12.5" fill="#2B3936">
        <text x="612" y="140">Your text or image.</text>
        <text x="612" y="162">Padding is inside the</text>
        <text x="612" y="180">border and takes the</text>
        <text x="612" y="198">background colour.</text>
        <text x="612" y="226">Margin is outside the</text>
        <text x="612" y="244">border and is always</text>
        <text x="612" y="262">transparent.</text>
      </g>

      <g stroke="#5A6B67" stroke-width="1.5">
        <line x1="212" y1="308" x2="468" y2="308"/>
        <line x1="212" y1="303" x2="212" y2="313"/><line x1="468" y1="303" x2="468" y2="313"/>
        <line x1="150" y1="326" x2="530" y2="326"/>
        <line x1="150" y1="321" x2="150" y2="331"/><line x1="530" y1="321" x2="530" y2="331"/>
      </g>
      <text x="340" y="303" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#5A6B67">content-box width</text>
      <text x="340" y="339" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="11" fill="#0B5D51" font-weight="600">border-box width — what you actually want</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>Four layers. The two measurements at the bottom are the whole reason <code>box-sizing</code> exists.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>content</b><span>The text or image itself.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>padding</b><span>Space <em>inside</em> the border. Takes the element's background colour. Use it to stop text touching the edge.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>border</b><span>A line around the padding. Has a width, style and colour.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>margin</b><span>Space <em>outside</em> the border. Always transparent. Use it to push other elements away.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">Padding or margin?</p><p><strong>Padding</strong> when the space should be part of the element — inside its background, inside its clickable area. <strong>Margin</strong> when the space is between this element and something else.</p><p>A button's inner breathing room is padding. The gap to the next button is margin — or better, <code>gap</code> on the parent, once you reach section 09.</p></div>

  <h3>The default that surprises everyone</h3>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.box</span> {
  <span class="at">width</span>: <span class="st">200px</span>;
  <span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">20px</span>;
  <span class="at">border</span>: <span class="st">5px solid black</span>;
}</div>

  <p>How wide is that box on screen? Not 200px. By default <code>width</code> sets the <strong>content</strong> width, and padding and border are added on top:</p>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="o">200  content</span>
<span class="o">+ 40  padding  (20 left + 20 right)</span>
<span class="o">+ 10  border   (5 left + 5 right)</span>
<span class="o">─────</span>
<span class="o">250px on screen</span></div>

  <p>This is <code>box-sizing: content-box</code>, the default, and it is a design decision from 1996 that almost nobody wants. Set a 25% width on four boxes, add any padding, and your row overflows.</p>

  <h3>The fix, which every project uses</h3>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="cm">/* put this at the top of every stylesheet you write */</span>
<span class="tg">*, *::before, *::after</span> {
  <span class="at">box-sizing</span>: <span class="st">border-box</span>;
}</div>

  <p>Now <code>width: 200px</code> means <strong>200px on screen, total</strong>, with padding and border eating into it rather than adding to it. Every CSS framework in existence does this. Do it in your first three lines and never think about it again.</p>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — see both, side by side</h4>
    <p>Add two boxes with the same width and padding, one of each sizing mode, then measure them in DevTools.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.a</span>, <span class="tg">.b</span> { <span class="at">width</span>: <span class="st">200px</span>; <span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">20px</span>; <span class="at">border</span>: <span class="st">5px solid</span>; <span class="at">margin-bottom</span>: <span class="st">1rem</span>; }
<span class="tg">.a</span> { <span class="at">box-sizing</span>: <span class="st">content-box</span>; }   <span class="cm">/* 250px wide */</span>
<span class="tg">.b</span> { <span class="at">box-sizing</span>: <span class="st">border-box</span>; }    <span class="cm">/* 200px wide  */</span></div>

  <h3>Shorthand, and the order</h3>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">10px</span>;                  <span class="cm">/* all four sides */</span>
<span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">10px 20px</span>;             <span class="cm">/* vertical | horizontal */</span>
<span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">10px 20px 30px</span>;        <span class="cm">/* top | horizontal | bottom */</span>
<span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">10px 20px 30px 40px</span>;   <span class="cm">/* top right bottom left — clockwise */</span></div>
  <p>Four values run clockwise from the top. The mnemonic everyone uses is <strong>TRouBLe</strong> — Top, Right, Bottom, Left. <code>margin</code> and <code>border-width</code> follow the same pattern.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">One shorthand worth memorising</p><p><code>margin: 0 auto;</code> — zero top and bottom, automatic left and right. <code>auto</code> splits the leftover horizontal space equally, which centres a block element that has a width. It's the oldest centring trick in CSS and still the right answer for a page container.</p></div>

  <div class="quiz">
    <div class="q"><b>Q3</b>Four cards, each <code>width: 25%</code> with <code>padding: 1rem</code>, in a row. They overflow. Why, and what's the one-line fix?</div>
    <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="a">
      <p>With <code>content-box</code>, each card is 25% <em>plus</em> 2rem of padding. Four of them come to 100% plus 8rem, which doesn't fit.</p>
      <p>Fix: <code>*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }</code>. Now 25% includes the padding and they fit exactly.</p>
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 04 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 05 ================= -->
<section id="s5">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">05</span><h2>Margin collapse</h2><span class="tagline">the genuinely weird one</span></div>

  <p>This one behaves in a way that looks like a bug, isn't, and wastes an afternoon of everyone's life exactly once.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">The rule</p><p><strong>Adjacent vertical margins collapse into one — the larger of the two.</strong> Not added together.</p></div>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.top</span>    { <span class="at">margin-bottom</span>: <span class="st">30px</span>; }
<span class="tg">.bottom</span> { <span class="at">margin-top</span>: <span class="st">20px</span>; }

<span class="cm">/* the gap between them is 30px, not 50px */</span></div>

  <p>Three things to know about it:</p>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>Vertical only</b><span>Horizontal margins never collapse. Two elements side by side with 20px each really are 40px apart.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>Parent and first child collapse too</b><span>A parent with no padding or border, whose first child has <code>margin-top: 40px</code>, gets pushed down by 40px itself — the margin "escapes" the parent. This is the one that looks broken.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>It doesn't happen in flex or grid</b><span>Margins on flex items and grid items never collapse. Which is one more reason modern layouts feel more predictable.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — reproduce the escaping margin</h4>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.parent</span> { <span class="at">background</span>: <span class="st">#E4EEEB</span>; }
<span class="tg">.parent h2</span> { <span class="at">margin-top</span>: <span class="st">40px</span>; }

<span class="cm">/* The gap appears ABOVE the parent's background, not inside it.
   Now add ONE of these to .parent and watch it move inside: */</span>
<span class="cm">/*   padding-top: 1px;  |  border-top: 1px solid;  |  display: flow-root;  */</span></div>

  <p><code>display: flow-root</code> is the clean modern fix — it creates a new block formatting context, which stops margins escaping, without adding a stray pixel of padding.</p>

  <div class="cue work"><p class="cue-k">How professionals sidestep the whole topic</p>
    <p><strong>Set margins in one direction only.</strong> Pick <code>margin-bottom</code> for everything (or <code>margin-top</code>, but pick one) and stick to it. If nothing ever has both, adjacent margins can't fight.</p>
    <p><strong>Or use <code>gap</code>.</strong> On a flex or grid container, <code>gap</code> spaces children with no margins at all — no collapse, no direction to remember, no last-child exception. This is why modern layouts barely use margins between siblings.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz">
    <div class="q"><b>Q4</b><code>.a</code> has <code>margin-bottom: 20px</code>. <code>.b</code>, directly after it, has <code>margin-top: 20px</code>. How big is the gap?</div>
    <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="a">
      <p><strong>20px.</strong> They collapse into one margin the size of the larger, and both are 20.</p>
      <p>People expect 40 and then start adding compensating negative margins, which is how a stylesheet begins its decline. Use <code>gap</code> instead.</p>
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 05 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 06 ================= -->
<section id="s6">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">06</span><h2>Display and normal flow</h2><span class="tagline">why things sit where they do</span></div>

  <p>Before any layout tool, the browser has a default way of arranging things called <strong>normal flow</strong>: block elements stack downwards, inline elements sit along a line and wrap. Everything else in this document is a deviation from that.</p>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>display: block</b><span>Starts on a new line, takes the full width available. <code>div</code>, <code>p</code>, <code>h1</code>, <code>section</code>. Width and height apply.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>display: inline</b><span>Sits within a line of text, only as wide as its content. <code>span</code>, <code>a</code>, <code>strong</code>. <strong>Width, height and vertical margins are ignored.</strong></span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>display: inline-block</b><span>Flows inline but accepts width, height and all margins. The old way of putting boxes in a row.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>display: none</b><span>Removed from the page entirely — takes no space, invisible to screen readers.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>display: flex</b><span>The element becomes a flex container and its <em>children</em> become flex items. Section 09.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>display: grid</b><span>Same idea, two-dimensional. Section 12.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">Setting height on an inline element does nothing</p><p>Try <code>a { height: 60px }</code> and nothing happens. Inline elements are sized by their content and the line box they sit in.</p><p>The fix is <code>display: inline-block</code>, or — much more often these days — make the parent a flex container, which turns every child into a flex item that accepts sizing.</p></div>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">display: none vs visibility: hidden vs opacity: 0</p><p><strong>none</strong> — gone, no space reserved, not read by screen readers. <strong>visibility: hidden</strong> — invisible but still occupies its space. <strong>opacity: 0</strong> — invisible, occupies space, and is <em>still clickable</em>, which produces genuinely baffling bugs.</p></div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — prove it to yourself</h4>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">span.tag</span> { <span class="at">background</span>: <span class="st">#F5C518</span>; <span class="at">height</span>: <span class="st">60px</span>; <span class="at">margin</span>: <span class="st">20px</span>; }
<span class="cm">/* height ignored, vertical margin ignored, horizontal margin applied */</span>

<span class="tg">span.tag</span> { <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">inline-block</span>; }
<span class="cm">/* now all of it works */</span></div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 06 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 07 ================= -->
<section id="s7">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">07</span><h2>Units</h2><span class="tagline">pick the right one and layouts stop fighting you</span></div>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>px</b><span>An absolute pixel. Predictable. Right for borders and the occasional fixed detail. Wrong for text, because it ignores a user's browser font-size setting.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>rem</b><span>Relative to the <strong>root</strong> font size — 16px by default. <code>1.5rem</code> = 24px. <strong>Your default for font sizes, padding, margins and gaps.</strong> Scales with user preference, and never compounds.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>em</b><span>Relative to <em>this element's own</em> font size. Useful for spacing that should scale with the text it sits next to — but it compounds through nesting, which catches people out.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>%</b><span>Relative to the parent — but to <em>which</em> dimension depends on the property. <code>width: 50%</code> is half the parent's width; <code>padding-top: 50%</code> is also based on the parent's <em>width</em>, not height.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>vw / vh</b><span>1% of viewport width / height. <code>100vh</code> is exactly one screen tall.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>dvh</b><span>Dynamic viewport height — accounts for mobile browser chrome appearing and disappearing. Use instead of <code>vh</code> for full-screen sections.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>ch</b><span>The width of the "0" character. <code>max-width: 65ch</code> is the classic readable line length.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>fr</b><span>A fraction of leftover space. Grid only. Section 12.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">The em compounding trap</p></div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.parent</span> { <span class="at">font-size</span>: <span class="st">1.2em</span>; }
<span class="tg">.child</span>  { <span class="at">font-size</span>: <span class="st">1.2em</span>; }   <span class="cm">/* 1.44em of the grandparent */</span>
<span class="tg">.grandchild</span> { <span class="at">font-size</span>: <span class="st">1.2em</span>; } <span class="cm">/* 1.728em — and growing */</span></div>
  <p>Nest three lists with <code>em</code> font sizes and the innermost text is enormous. <code>rem</code> always refers to the root, so it never compounds. Default to <code>rem</code>.</p>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">100vh is wrong on mobile</p><p>Mobile browsers hide and show the address bar as you scroll, so <code>100vh</code> is taller than the visible area and your "full screen" hero gets cut off. Use <code>100dvh</code>, or <code>min-height: 100svh</code> if you want the smallest safe height.</p></div>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">A sane default set</p><p>Text and spacing in <code>rem</code>. Borders in <code>px</code>. Layout widths in <code>%</code>, <code>fr</code> or <code>minmax()</code>. Reading widths in <code>ch</code>. Full-height sections in <code>dvh</code>. That covers essentially everything you'll build.</p></div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 07 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 08 ================= -->
<section id="s8">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">08</span><h2>Colour and custom properties</h2><span class="tagline">stop repeating yourself</span></div>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">#0B5D51</span>;                  <span class="cm">/* hex */</span>
<span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">rgb(11 93 81)</span>;            <span class="cm">/* modern space-separated syntax */</span>
<span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">rgb(11 93 81 / 50%)</span>;      <span class="cm">/* with alpha */</span>
<span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">hsl(172 79% 20%)</span>;         <span class="cm">/* hue saturation lightness */</span></div>

  <p><code>hsl</code> is worth knowing because it's the only one you can reason about by hand: keep the hue, drop the lightness, and you have a darker shade of the same colour. Producing a palette in hex means guessing.</p>

  <h3>Custom properties — real variables, in the browser</h3>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">:root</span> {
  <span class="at">--brand</span>: <span class="st">#0B5D51</span>;
  <span class="at">--paper</span>: <span class="st">#DDE3E0</span>;
  <span class="at">--space</span>: <span class="st">1rem</span>;
  <span class="at">--radius</span>: <span class="st">4px</span>;
}

<span class="tg">.button</span> {
  <span class="at">background</span>: <span class="st">var(--brand)</span>;
  <span class="at">padding</span>: <span class="st">var(--space) calc(var(--space) * 2)</span>;
  <span class="at">border-radius</span>: <span class="st">var(--radius)</span>;
}</div>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>Two dashes to define</b><span><code>--name: value</code>. <code>:root</code> is the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element, so anything defined there is available everywhere.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>var() to use</b><span><code>var(--name)</code>, with an optional fallback: <code>var(--name, #ccc)</code>.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>They inherit</b><span>Redefine one inside a component and everything beneath uses the new value. This is what makes theming trivial.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>They're live</b><span>Unlike Sass variables, these exist at runtime. JavaScript can change one and every rule using it updates instantly.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — build a dark mode in six lines</h4>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">:root</span> { <span class="at">--bg</span>: <span class="st">white</span>; <span class="at">--fg</span>: <span class="st">#111</span>; }

<span class="tg">@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</span> {
  <span class="tg">:root</span> { <span class="at">--bg</span>: <span class="st">#111</span>; <span class="at">--fg</span>: <span class="st">#eee</span>; }
}

<span class="tg">body</span> { <span class="at">background</span>: <span class="st">var(--bg)</span>; <span class="at">color</span>: <span class="st">var(--fg)</span>; }</div>

  <p>Change your operating system to dark mode and the page follows. Two colour definitions, no JavaScript, no class toggling.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">Name them by role, not appearance</p><p><code>--brand</code> and <code>--danger</code>, not <code>--green</code> and <code>--red</code>. When the design changes and your green becomes blue, a variable called <code>--green</code> holding blue is worse than no variable at all.</p></div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 08 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 09 ================= -->
<section id="s9">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">09</span><h2>Flexbox — the two axes</h2><span class="tagline">one dimension, done properly</span></div>

  <p>Flexbox arranges items along <strong>one line</strong> — a row or a column — and distributes space between them. If your problem is "put these things in a row and space them out", this is the tool.</p>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.container</span> {
  <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">flex</span>;
}</div>

  <p>That single line does a lot: every direct child becomes a <strong>flex item</strong>, they line up horizontally, they no longer collapse margins, and they shrink to fit rather than overflowing.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">Everything depends on the axes</p><p>The <strong>main axis</strong> runs in the direction of <code>flex-direction</code>. The <strong>cross axis</strong> is perpendicular to it. <code>justify-*</code> works along the main axis; <code>align-*</code> works along the cross axis.</p><p>Get that pair straight and Flexbox stops being trial and error.</p></div>

  <figure class="board">
