      <g font-family="IBM Plex Sans Condensed, sans-serif" font-weight="700" font-size="13" fill="#0F1A18">
        <text x="0" y="234">flex-direction: column  —  the axes swap over</text>
      </g>
      <rect x="0" y="248" width="200" height="86" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#0F1A18" stroke-width="2"/>
      <rect x="20" y="258" width="120" height="20" fill="#E4EEEB" stroke="#0B5D51" stroke-width="2"/>
      <rect x="20" y="282" width="120" height="20" fill="#E4EEEB" stroke="#0B5D51" stroke-width="2"/>
      <rect x="20" y="306" width="120" height="20" fill="#E4EEEB" stroke="#0B5D51" stroke-width="2"/>
      <line x1="160" y1="256" x2="160" y2="328" stroke="#F5C518" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#mA)"/>
      <line x1="20" y1="242" x2="140" y2="242" stroke="#0B5D51" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#mB)"/>
      <text x="216" y="276" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#0F1A18">Now <tspan font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#8A6D08" font-weight="600">justify-content</tspan> moves things vertically</text>
      <text x="216" y="298" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#0F1A18">and <tspan font-family="IBM Plex Mono, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#0B5D51" font-weight="600">align-items</tspan> moves them horizontally.</text>
      <text x="216" y="322" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#5A6B67">The property names never change — the axes do.</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>This is the whole mental model. Nearly every Flexbox mistake is applying the right property to the wrong axis.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-direction</b><span><code>row</code> (default), <code>column</code>, <code>row-reverse</code>, <code>column-reverse</code>.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-wrap</b><span><code>nowrap</code> (default — items shrink rather than wrap) or <code>wrap</code> (they move to a new line).</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>gap</b><span>Space between items. Replaces margins entirely, with no last-child exception to work around. Use it.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — a navigation bar in five lines</h4>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">nav</span> {
  <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">flex</span>;
  <span class="at">gap</span>: <span class="st">1.5rem</span>;
  <span class="at">align-items</span>: <span class="st">center</span>;
  <span class="at">justify-content</span>: <span class="st">space-between</span>;
}</div>
  <p>Logo on the left, links on the right, everything vertically centred, evenly spaced. That layout used to take floats, clearfixes and a bad afternoon.</p>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 09 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 10 ================= -->
<section id="s10">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">10</span><h2>Flexbox — alignment</h2><span class="tagline">the properties you'll use daily</span></div>

  <h3>Along the main axis — justify-content</h3>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-start</b><span>Bunched at the start. The default.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>center</b><span>Bunched in the middle.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-end</b><span>Bunched at the end.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>space-between</b><span>First at the start, last at the end, equal gaps between. The nav bar classic.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>space-around</b><span>Equal space around each item, so edge gaps are half the size of inner ones.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>space-evenly</b><span>Every gap identical, including the edges. Usually what people mean when they reach for <code>space-around</code>.</span></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Across the cross axis — align-items</h3>
  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>stretch</b><span>Items fill the container's height. <strong>The default</strong> — this is why flex children often look taller than expected.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>center</b><span>Vertically centred in a row. This is the one you'll use most.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-start / flex-end</b><span>Top or bottom of the container.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>baseline</b><span>Text baselines line up, even when font sizes differ. Underrated for labels next to inputs.</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">Perfect centring, finally</p></div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.centre</span> {
  <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">flex</span>;
  <span class="at">justify-content</span>: <span class="st">center</span>;   <span class="cm">/* horizontal, in a row */</span>
  <span class="at">align-items</span>: <span class="st">center</span>;       <span class="cm">/* vertical, in a row */</span>
  <span class="at">min-height</span>: <span class="st">100dvh</span>;
}

<span class="cm">/* or the shorthand for both */</span>
<span class="tg">.centre</span> { <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">flex</span>; <span class="at">place-items</span>: <span class="st">center</span>; }</div>
  <p>Vertical centring was genuinely hard for about fifteen years and generated an entire genre of blog post. It is now two lines.</p>

  <h3>Overriding one item — align-self</h3>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.item-3</span> { <span class="at">align-self</span>: <span class="st">flex-end</span>; }   <span class="cm">/* just this one drops to the bottom */</span></div>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">The auto-margin trick</p><p>On a flex item, <code>margin-left: auto</code> absorbs all remaining space and pushes that item — and everything after it — to the far end.</p><p>Perfect for "logo, some links, then a login button pinned right" without splitting the markup into wrapper divs.</p></div>

  <div class="quiz">
    <div class="q"><b>Q5</b>You set <code>flex-direction: column</code> and now <code>align-items: center</code> centres things horizontally instead of vertically. Is this a bug?</div>
    <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="a">
      <p><strong>No — it's the definition working correctly.</strong> <code>align-items</code> always works on the cross axis. In a column, the main axis is vertical, so the cross axis is horizontal.</p>
      <p>To centre vertically in a column you want <code>justify-content: center</code>. Nothing about the properties changed; the axes rotated.</p>
    </div></details>
  </div>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 10 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 11 ================= -->
<section id="s11">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">11</span><h2>Flexbox — grow, shrink, basis</h2><span class="tagline">how space gets divided</span></div>

  <p>Those three properties on the <em>items</em> control how leftover space is shared out, and they're bundled into one shorthand.</p>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">&lt;grow&gt; &lt;shrink&gt; &lt;basis&gt;</span>;

<span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">0 1 auto</span>;    <span class="cm">/* the default: don't grow, do shrink, size by content */</span>
<span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">1</span>;          <span class="cm">/* shorthand for 1 1 0% — grow to fill, ignore content width */</span>
<span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">1 1 200px</span>;   <span class="cm">/* start at 200px, then grow and shrink from there */</span>
<span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">0 0 200px</span>;   <span class="cm">/* exactly 200px, never flexes. A fixed sidebar. */</span></div>

  <div class="mention">
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-grow</b><span>How much of the <em>leftover</em> space this item takes, relative to its siblings. <code>0</code> means none.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-shrink</b><span>How much it gives up when there isn't enough room. <code>1</code> by default, <code>0</code> to forbid shrinking.</span></div>
    <div class="mrow"><b>flex-basis</b><span>The starting size before growing or shrinking. <code>auto</code> means "use the content or the width property".</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="dothis">
    <h4>Do this now — the classic app layout</h4>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.layout</span>  { <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">flex</span>; <span class="at">gap</span>: <span class="st">1rem</span>; }
<span class="tg">.sidebar</span> { <span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">0 0 240px</span>; }   <span class="cm">/* fixed, never moves */</span>
<span class="tg">.main</span>    { <span class="at">flex</span>: <span class="st">1</span>; }           <span class="cm">/* takes everything else */</span></div>
  <p>Drag the browser window narrower. The sidebar stays exactly 240px, the main area absorbs every pixel of change. Two declarations.</p>

  <div class="cue"><p class="cue-k">Ratios, not percentages</p><p>Three items with <code>flex: 1</code>, <code>flex: 2</code>, <code>flex: 1</code> divide the free space in a 1:2:1 ratio. It's proportional, so you never have to make the numbers add up to anything.</p></div>

  <div class="cue watch"><p class="cue-k">The min-width bug that catches everyone</p>
    <p>A flex item won't shrink below its content's minimum size — because <code>min-width</code> defaults to <code>auto</code> for flex items, not <code>0</code>. So one long unbroken string, a wide table, or a <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> block will blow your layout out past the viewport and give you a horizontal scrollbar.</p>
    <p>The fix, which looks like magic until you know why:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.flex-item</span> {
  <span class="at">min-width</span>: <span class="st">0</span>;        <span class="cm">/* now it may shrink below its content */</span>
  <span class="at">overflow</span>: <span class="st">hidden</span>;   <span class="cm">/* or overflow-wrap: break-word for text */</span>
}</div>
  <p>In a column, the same problem appears as <code>min-height: auto</code>. If a flex layout is mysteriously overflowing, this is the first thing to try.</p>

  <label class="done-check"><input type="checkbox" class="chk"><span class="dbox"></span><span>Section 11 complete</span></label>
</section>

<!-- ================= 12 ================= -->
<section id="s12">
  <div class="shead"><span class="chip">12</span><h2>Grid — the anatomy</h2><span class="tagline">two dimensions at once</span></div>

  <p>Flexbox handles one line. Grid handles rows <strong>and</strong> columns simultaneously, so items line up in both directions — which Flexbox genuinely cannot do once things wrap.</p>

  <div class="cmd"><span class="tg">.grid</span> {
  <span class="at">display</span>: <span class="st">grid</span>;
  <span class="at">grid-template-columns</span>: <span class="st">200px 1fr 1fr</span>;
  <span class="at">grid-template-rows</span>: <span class="st">auto 1fr</span>;
  <span class="at">gap</span>: <span class="st">1rem</span>;
}</div>

  <figure class="board">
